  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="contract" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.1 · a small portable boundary</span>
        <h2>Describe every capability, invoke exactly one.</h2>
        <p>Every provider component exports two operations. <code>describe</code> returns its strict manifest. <code>invoke</code> receives one capability identifier and one JSON object, then returns a typed success or failure. A command-enabled provider may add the optional pure <code>resolve-command</code> export.</p>
      </div>

      <pre><code><span class="code-purple">world</span> provider {
  <span class="code-purple">export</span> describe: func() -&gt; string;
  <span class="code-purple">export</span> invoke: func(
    capability: string,
    input-json: string
  ) -&gt; string;
}</code></pre>

      <div class="build-pipeline">
        <div class="build-step"><span>1</span><strong>Implement</strong><small>typed Rust provider</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div class="build-step"><span>2</span><strong>Compile</strong><small><code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code></small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div class="build-step"><span>3</span><strong>Inspect</strong><small>declared WIT imports</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→</i>
        <div class="build-step build-step-final"><span>4</span><strong>Load</strong><small>operator-owned host</small></div>
      </div>

      <p>A component can’t download a native runtime extension or invent a host import. Its requirements are visible before it runs, and the host supplies exactly those interfaces or rejects it.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="hosts" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.2 · two deliberately different hosts</span>
        <h2>The linker is the first permission boundary.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="run-mode-cards">
        <article class="run-mode-current">
          <div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-current"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Direct</span><span><code>dekopon-run</code></span></div>
          <h3>Import-free local computation</h3>
          <p>The linker supplies nothing. Components declaring network imports fail to load, and manifests may contain only read-only effects.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>no filesystem, network, environment, clock, random source, or credential</li>
            <li>pure transforms and provider development</li>
            <li>raw untrusted output, not authorization evidence</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
        <article class="run-mode-future">
          <div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-current"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Broker</span><span><code>dekopon-brokerd</code></span></div>
          <h3>Permissioned provider effects</h3>
          <p>The privileged host links an exact project-owned HTTP or storage interface after Cedar allows an invocation and the broker attaches constraints.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>no WASI, raw sockets, host paths, environment, or guest-visible secrets</li>
            <li>exact network or storage scope, quotas, bytes, and deadline</li>
            <li>sanitized evidence and durable audit linkage</li>
          </ul>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-decision">
        <strong>Declaring an import isn’t receiving authority.</strong>
        <p>A component can say it needs <code>dekopon:http@1.0.0</code> or <code>dekopon:storage@0.1.0</code>. Only the broker can connect that import to a constrained native service for one authorized invocation.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="fresh" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.3 · clean state per operation</span>
        <h2>Reuse the compiled code, reset the dangerous state.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="store-lifecycle">
        <div class="store-shared">
          <span>retained for speed</span>
          <div><strong>Wasmtime engine</strong><small>shared runtime configuration</small></div>
          <div><strong>compiled component</strong><small>in process · optional disk cache across restarts</small></div>
          <div><strong>capability routes</strong><small>trusted provider map</small></div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">each invoke ↓</i>
        <div class="store-fresh-row">
          <div><span>fresh</span><strong>Store</strong><small>fuel + limits + host state</small></div>
          <div><span>fresh</span><strong>instance</strong><small>new guest globals</small></div>
          <div><span>fresh</span><strong>memory</strong><small>dies with the call</small></div>
          <div><span>single use</span><strong>grant</strong><small>consumed by execution</small></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>Description and command resolution run with HTTP and storage disabled, even in the broker host. Provider code can’t use a host service while advertising or rewriting what it can do.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="http" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.4 · network access one call at a time</span>
        <h2>The provider builds a request inside a smaller envelope.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="provider-envelope">
        <div><small>provider asks</small><strong><code>POST api.github.com</code></strong><span>path + bounded body</span></div>
        <div><small>authorization binds</small><strong>host · method · 2 calls</strong><span>request/response bytes + deadline</span></div>
        <div><small>native engine enforces</small><strong>pinned DNS · no redirects</strong><span>no proxy · no decompression</span></div>
        <div><small>evidence keeps</small><strong>method · host · status</strong><span>byte counts, never bodies</span></div>
      </div>

      <p>Constraints can narrow the native host ceilings and never widen them. Plain HTTP is rejected except for explicitly authorized literal loopback tests. DNS results are validated and pinned before connection.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="storage" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.5 · durable state without host paths</span>
        <h2>The broker gives storage a namespace, not a filesystem.</h2>
        <p>The current broker provides exact JSONL and durable-file imports. A component names logical records or files; it never receives the physical root, the namespace key, or any filesystem access.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="provider-envelope">
        <div><small>provider asks</small><strong>JSONL or durable files</strong><span>one declared interface</span></div>
        <div><small>authorization binds</small><strong>provider · action · chat scope</strong><span>access mode + limits</span></div>
        <div><small>native engine enforces</small><strong>quota · lease · transaction</strong><span>no-follow filesystem operations</span></div>
        <div><small>commit rule</small><strong>valid successful response</strong><span>otherwise abort the overlay</span></div>
      </div>

      <p>HTTP and storage authority can’t coexist in one v1 capability. Storage mutations commit only after a valid successful provider response. The optional <code>memory-chat</code> component uses JSONL to record delivered turns and expose <code>memory recent</code> and <code>memory search</code> under fresh authorization.</p>

      <p>The public <code>dekopon-provider-sdk-testkit</code> lets a provider test its compiled component against this real storage host. It skips Cedar and the constraint catalog by minting test authorization; namespace derivation, quotas, leases, transactions, and the Wasmtime path remain the production implementations.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-warning">
        <strong>Durable doesn’t mean encrypted or automatically remembered.</strong>
        <p>Dekopon doesn’t encrypt stored text at rest, doesn’t replay it into prompts, and has no deletion or export UX. The namespace key protects derivation and commitments; it isn’t a content-encryption key.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="credential" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.6 · broker-held secrets</span>
        <h2>The broker injects the credential.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="credential-injection-flow">
        <div><span>owner-only store</span><strong><code>github-comment-token</code></strong><small>destination: api.github.com</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">selected by trusted agent context →</i>
        <div><span>guest request</span><strong>headers validated</strong><small>guest authorization header refused</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">then →</i>
        <div><span>native HTTP engine</span><strong>injects Authorization</strong><small>only at the bound destination</small></div>
      </div>

      <p>The value stays out of prompts, guest imports, evidence, spans, and normal logs. The native engine injects it only after validating the request. An allowed endpoint can still hand it back in the response, and the component will see it — destination binding isn’t an endpoint confidentiality guarantee. Audit records the credential’s owner-authored symbolic name, so two organizations’ effects stay distinguishable without recording either token.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="scope" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>04.7 · honest isolation claims</span>
        <h2>The provider is contained; the host isn’t.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="truth-label-cards">
        <article><div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-current"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Enforced</span></div><h3>Guest boundaries</h3><p>Declared imports, fresh stores, and the memory, fuel, and deadline ceilings are checked in code.</p></article>
        <article><div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-current"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Separated</span></div><h3>Authority boundaries</h3><p>The gateway and direct runner exclude privileged broker crates. Only the separate broker owns policy, credentials, and effect execution.</p></article>
        <article><div><span class="almanac-status almanac-status-direction"><span aria-hidden="true"></span>Not claimed</span></div><h3>Compromised host defense</h3><p>Wasm doesn’t protect a broker machine, a toolchain, or a dependency set that’s already compromised.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="article-callout callout-decision">
        <strong>Providers can move independently without becoming plugins.</strong>
        <p>GitHub, MediaWiki, Python, ripgrep, and Turso SQL now ship from separate repositories. Their release cadence and dependency graphs are independent; their components still enter through the same manifest, WIT, broker policy, constraints, and fresh-store path.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Ready to build one? <a href="/guides/provider-sdk/"><strong>Build, test, and run a provider with the pinned {{ release.tag }} Rust SDK</strong></a>.</p>

